===ZKP1 v1 self-contained reveal — end-to-end validation log (Groth16 + PLONK, with tx_base binding)===

The historical Pair A / Pair B / Pair P examples above are all ZKP1 v0 — the verification key is distributed out-of-band, and an auditor needs both the on-chain reveal and a separately-published <code>verification_key.json</code> to fully validate the proof. ZKP1 v1 closes that gap: when the encoder is invoked with <code>--vkey</code>, the verification key bytes are embedded inside the canonical payload so the on-chain reveal alone is sufficient to verify the proof end-to-end. Because the vk is part of the canonical byte string hashed to <code>commit32</code>, swapping the vk after publication invalidates the OP_RETURN commitment match — the consensus-relevant binding extends to the verifier material itself.

In addition to embedding the vk, the v1 e2e flow now binds every proof to the funding transaction's tx_base sighash (<code>tx_binding</code> public input, defined in §"Phase 1: Base Transaction Binding" above). This mirrors the PQC carrier's signature-over-tx_base model and is independently checked by snarkjs at verify time and by spvnode at scan time.

The v1 reveal flow is exercised by <code>contrib/zk_carrier/scripts/v1_reveal_e2e.sh</code>, which:

# constructs a synthetic TX_C base (single-vin, single-vout to the funded address <code>DDMpdcTrWnZT38tRMebbYzCSAgLSnVMqvr</code>; live UTXO state at <code>https://chain.so/address/DOGE/DDMpdcTrWnZT38tRMebbYzCSAgLSnVMqvr</code>) and computes <code>tx_base_sighash = sighash32(BASE_UNSIGNED, signer_p2pkh_spk, in_idx=0, hashtype=SIGHASH_ALL)</code> via <code>such -c tx_sighash32</code>, top-byte-zeroed to fit BN254;
# generates a real Groth16/PLONK range-proof against the in-tree <code>contrib/zk_carrier/circuits/range_proof.circom</code> (witness <code>amount=50000</code> in <code>[0, 1000000]</code>, <code>tx_binding</code> set to the value from step 1) via <code>witness_helper.py --vkey ... --proof-system &lt;groth16|plonk&gt; --tx-binding-hex ...</code>;
# encodes the ZKP1 v1 payload (version byte = <code>0x01</code>; trailing <code>VK_LEN4 || VK</code> block);
# attaches OP_RETURN + P2SH carrier outputs through <code>such -c zk_add_commit_and_carrier_tx</code>;
# constructs a synthetic TX_R, attaches each <code>zk_carrier_part_scriptsig</code> via <code>such -c set_scriptsig</code>, then reassembles the payload with <code>such -c zk_extract_carrier</code> (which now also emits <code>zk_vk_len</code> / <code>zk_vk_hex</code> for v1 reveals);
# materializes <code>(public.json, proof.json, vk.json)</code> from the extracted bytes only — no out-of-band file is read at this step — and runs <code>snarkjs &lt;system&gt; verify</code>;
# recomputes <code>tx_base_sighash</code> from TX_C and asserts equality with <code>public_inputs[2]</code> of the recovered payload — emitting the same <code>[zk-commit] tx_binding match</code> log line that <code>spvnode</code> emits in production;
# performs a replay-attack tamper test: builds an alternate funding tx with a different <code>vout[0].value</code>, swaps the <code>tx_binding</code> public input to that alternate sighash, and asserts that <code>snarkjs verify</code> rejects the lifted proof under the new binding.

